---
/* WHAT YOU HAVE ALREADY SEARCHED, KEPT PAGE BY PAGE.

   David: «i want to be able to store the page im up to, or pages ive done,
   not just a yes/no/partial» — and, on what this atlas is for: «this was
   always a geneology tool, now it just isnt a info tool it actually give
   something».

   RANGES, BECAUSE THAT IS HOW THE WORK IS ACTUALLY DONE. Nobody searches a
   register and comes away with a boolean. They come away with «I did images
   1 to 47, then the light went». So the unit stored is a set of ranges, the
   input accepts what a person would write — «1-47, 60-72» — and the thing
   the page shows back is where to start again.

   NO INVENTED DENOMINATOR. This atlas does NOT know how long a volume is: a
   volume row carries a waypoint, a title and years, and the only image counts
   in the data are collection-level aggregates like 283,815,879 for Find a
   Grave. So there is no progress bar until the reader types the total they
   can see on the film themselves, and when they do it is labelled as theirs.
   A percentage this map made up would be the one thing it exists not to do.

   LOCALSTORAGE, AND THIS TIME THAT IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. Elsewhere in
   this project localStorage is used for conveniences, with the note that it
   «is a convenience and not a record». Page ranges ARE a record — somebody
   may spend months building them — and site data gets cleared, private
   windows forget, browsers evict under pressure, laptops get replaced. So
   export is a first-class verb here rather than a footnote, and the pages
   that show progress say plainly where it lives.

   THE ROW SHAPE BORROWS FROM THE ARCHIVE KIT, which already had to answer
   «what is being done» for eight family archives: `covers` is a list of
   stable ids, `since` is when it was raised. Keeping the same two words means
   a reader's own progress can be exported into an archive's worklist.json
   instead of being a second, incompatible record of the same work. `owner`
   and `state` are dropped: there is one owner here, and a range says more
   than a state does.

   ONE VOLUME, SEVERAL FAMILIES, DIFFERENT WORK.

   David: «if I'm researching multiple families… searching for Blažević in
   Senj may be different progress than if I am doing Kosina in Senj.»

   He is right, and his own archives prove it: inside the Blažević archive,
   Kranj–Šmartin marriages 1780–1815 was worked for KOSINAR and 1816–1855 for
   KOSINA. Same parish, same shelf, different line. Across all 1,349 rows in
   the eight archives, 519 describe hunting a named family and 72 describe
   walking a book with no name in sight.

   BECAUSE THOSE ARE TWO DIFFERENT KINDS OF WORK, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM
   GENERALISES.
     · WALKING a register — reading every entry — is true for every family
       ever after. Pages 1–174 walked are walked.
     · SCANNING for a name is true for that name and nothing else. «1–47
       scanned for Blažević» says nothing whatever about Kosina.
   Version 1 stored one `done` per volume and quietly treated the second as
   the first, which overstates the work on every line but the one it was
   done for — and the entire value of this record is knowing what is still
   left to do.

   So a mark now carries `walked`, shared by everyone, and `by[line]`, which
   is not. What a reader sees for a line is the union of the two.

   MIGRATION CLAIMS NOTHING. Marks made under v1 have no line and were mostly
   made while hunting one family, so promoting them to `walked` would assert
   coverage for families that were never looked for. They go into a single
   line instead, named «My research so far», which is renameable — David
   chose this over the promotion when asked. Nothing is lost and nothing is
   claimed.
*/
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<script is:inline src={u("/js/progress-kit.js")}></script>
